battery-out/

# Agent QA/review output — Playwright-MCP scratch and local screenshot evidence
# that issues reference by repo path (e.g. #2710, #2711). Never committed.
# These were untracked-but-not-ignored, which made the shared main checkout
# permanently DIRTY and blocked every git op via the M4 hub-state gate (#2238).
.playwright-mcp/
review-artifacts/
# Content-addressed kind-embedding index cache (#1695 Task 3) — the .npz
# files are gitignored; the directory convention (.gitkeep) is committed.
data/kind_index/*
!data/kind_index/.gitkeep

# #2943: the durable pre-wipe snapshot sidecar. `rebuild_all` writes it next to
# the event log it was given, and `tortoise rebuild --dir` defaults to "." — so
# it can land in a working tree. It holds graph-only Point contents and :Batch
# markers, which must never reach git history. It is transient (written before
# the wipe, removed after a completed replay).
.tortoise-prewipe-snapshot.json
.tortoise-prewipe-*.tmp

# #9190: the journal POSITION cache, written beside whatever journal it
# describes (`<journal>.position` — the suffix is appended to the journal's own
# name, so it is not tied to `*.jsonl`; `tortoise ingest --log graph.events`
# writes `graph.events.position`). Same exposure as the snapshot above:
# `ingest` / `rebuild` default the event log to `events.jsonl` in the CWD, so a
# routine run in a checkout root leaves one here. Derived state — a stale or
# deleted sidecar costs a recount, never a wrong cached number — and nothing
# else in the tree produces this suffix, so it must never be committed.
*.position
.venv
.pytest_cache/

# #5373: the SDK rename table is GENERATED from `sdk.py` (AST line numbers) and two
# docs, and it is a function of a file every lane edits. Committing it made any two
# concurrent `sdk.py` PRs unmergeable: measured 2026-09-26, it was the sole conflict
# in 8 of the 44 unclean PRs, and normalising `sdk.py:\d+` made the two sides
# byte-identical — git conflicted on two files that differed only by a number nobody
# reads. It is NOT unioned (a union of two regenerations duplicates rows and matches
# neither side); it is generated ON DEMAND instead:
#
#   uv run python tools/sdk_rename_table.py            # write it
#   uv run python tools/sdk_rename_table.py --check    # verify a local copy
#
# `tests/test_sdk_rename_table.py` renders it to a temp file and asserts it against
# independent AST/doc oracles, so the mapping is still checked on every run — only
# the committed copy (and its conflicts) is gone. The MCP half
# (`docs/product/mcp-rename-table.md`) is a separate, still-committed artifact.
docs/product/sdk-rename-table.md

# #9790: `tools/batch_review.py prepare` writes its cached diffs, manifest and
# prompt to `.batch-review/` in the CWD (that is the documented invocation), and
# nothing removed them — a stray artifact in a repo whose worktrees are shared
# across lanes. Derived state: it is re-created by `prepare`, so it must never be
# committed.
.batch-review/

# #10220: `tools/worktree_report.py` writes a lane's durable report to
# `WORKTREE-REPORT.md` at the WORKTREE ROOT, with its raw suite output under
# `.worktree-report/`. That is lane-local evidence about a working tree — it
# describes a branch, not the product — and the merge path reads it from disk
# (agent-infra#2977), so it must never be committed. Derived: every `run` rewrites
# it, and `check` re-derives the counts from the logs it cites.
/WORKTREE-REPORT.md
/.worktree-report/
